.TH CSTR_INIT 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cstr_init \- initialise a declared cstr with the default allocators
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

cstr_init(s, initial);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro initialises the cstr variable s with the content you give,
through cstring_create(). You must declare s first. If the creation
fails, the macro calls _ccol_fatal_err().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s        cstr lvalue to initialise (you must declare it first); the macro
         assigns it once, after the string is created
initial  Initial content (C string or NULL for empty string)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A failed allocation ends in _ccol_fatal_err(). If you want to handle an
error yourself, call cstring_create() directly
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cstr_declare(s);
cstr_init(s, "hello");
cstr_destroy(s);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstr_init_mp (3),
.BR cstr_construct (3),
.BR cstring_create (3)
